module Native

{-
   The instructions that are one machine's rather than an operation the language has - x86's, and
   nothing that is not one.

   Everything in `Core` is portable: a name there means the same thing on every target, and where a
   target has no instruction for it a backend writes what it does have. **This file is the
   exception**, and there is one of it per processor family. `Intrinsic/X86.yana` is x86's; an ARM
   one joins it beside rather than inside it, under its own namespace and its own attributes, and
   nothing outside this directory declares an instruction at all.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why `Native`, and why a namespace inside it

   A file of module `Native`, two directories deep, which is what `module Native` above says
   (Analysis-Modules.md §2.1). `Native` is the unsafe half of the prelude and the one module that is
   **not** implicitly imported, so naming an instruction costs an `import Native` - which is exactly
   the statement a program should have to make to leave portable ground. `Core` is implicitly
   imported and is therefore the wrong place: a name reachable by writing nothing must mean the same
   thing on every target.

   `X86.` is a namespace and not a module - doc/spec/modules.md. A module would give these a `pub`
   boundary they have no interface behind, a second import every user of them would write, and a
   coherence unit; what a prefix is wanted for here is naming and nothing else. It has to be there
   because a reader of `Digest/Hardware.sha.yana` must be able to tell an instruction from a
   function: `sha1Rounds(abcd, e, 0)` reads as a library call and is a single opcode with a trailing
   byte, and `X86.sha1Rounds(abcd, e, 0)` says which manual to open.

   The names are the machine's, spelled the way this language spells names. Anyone reading a body
   that uses them is reading against Intel's manual, and a name invented here would only stand
   between the two.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why availability is an attribute per declaration and not a selector on the file name

   Every declaration below carries the target it needs, and there are three different answers in one
   file: `@platform(x64)` for what an x86-64 build always has, `@platform(v3)` for what the AVX tier
   supplies, and `@platform(sha)` for what an extension no level implies answers for. **A declaration
   an attribute excluded is never resolved**, which is the whole of what the gating owes: a build
   without the instruction must not resolve a body naming it, and it cannot, because there is no
   declaration for such a body to name.

   A file-name selector (`X86.sha.yana`) says one thing about *every* declaration in a file at once,
   and three answers is exactly why that is the wrong shape here - `crc32` and `vzeroupper` have
   nothing to do with the SHA extension, and a file selected on `sha` deletes them from every build
   that does not claim it. One attribute per declaration says which instruction needs what, and
   adding a second extension adds a declaration rather than a file.

   What is still a *file* is code written **in terms of** these - `Digest/Hardware.sha.yana` and its
   `nosha` half, two implementations of the same two names. That is a body rather than a
   declaration, and the distinction is load-bearing: a body kept behind a compile-time
   `if hasShaExtension()` leaves the untaken arm **resolved**. It is IR, it reaches lowering in any
   build with the IR optimizer off (`-no-opt`, which every fixture in this tree is also compiled
   with), and the backend then refuses instructions the target cannot encode. See `targetSelector`
   in compiler/compiler/settings.cpp, which answers both spellings so that an attribute and a file
   name can never disagree about what a target is called.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Why there is nothing portable behind them

   Every other machine-specific operation this compiler knows about is an *arrangement of bits* a
   backend can write out longhand: `pext` is a loop, `lzcnt` is a scan and a subtraction, `movbe` is
   a load and a reversal. Nothing here is. `sha256rnds2` is two rounds of a hash and `crc32` is a
   named polynomial, so a backend that expanded either would hold a second implementation of a
   published algorithm inside the compiler, tested by nothing and free to drift from the library's.
   There is therefore no expansion behind any of these at any tier, and what stands in for them is a
   different implementation of the same function selected the same way: `Digest/Hardware.nosha.yana`
   for the hashes, and the byte table in `Digest/Crc.yana` for the checksum.
-}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `@platform(x64)` - what an x86-64 target has by having been x86-64.
   ==========================================================================================

   The CRC-32C step. SSE4.2, and therefore x86-64-v2, and therefore this backend's floor:
   `kFeatureBaseline` *is* v2 (codegen/x64/target.h), so there is no feature name beside the
   architecture here and no tier below this one to fall back to.

   `X86.crc32(remainder, chunk)` folds `chunk` into the running remainder under the **Castagnoli**
   polynomial `0x11EDC6F41`, reflected. That is the polynomial in the silicon and there is no
   parameter for another: the IEEE polynomial that zip, gzip, PNG and Ethernet mean is a different
   function this instruction cannot compute, and a caller who wants it wants the table in
   `Digest/Crc.yana`. The two live side by side there, and the note above them is where the
   difference is written down rather than here.

   **One width, and it is sixty-four.** The machine has `crc32 r32/r64, r/m8/16/32/64`; what a
   checksum over a run of bytes wants is the widest of those, and the narrower ones are not
   declared because nothing above the backend can select one - the lower IR has no scalar below
   `Int32`, so a byte-wide chunk cannot be told apart from a 32-bit one. A tail of fewer than eight
   bytes reaches the table instead, which costs a handful of lookups once per message and nothing
   per block.

   **The remainder is at the chunk's width rather than at thirty-two**, which is what the machine
   does: the 64-bit form reads its remainder from the low half of its destination and writes a
   zero-extended answer, so the `widen` and the `truncate` a caller writes around it are conversions
   the instruction already performs and both fold away. `crcWide` in `Digest/Crc.native.yana` is
   that caller and is where it shows.
-}
@platform(x64) pub fn X86.crc32(remainder: U64, chunk: U64) -> U64

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `@platform(v3)` - the AVX tier.
   ==========================================================================================

   `vzeroupper` - the vector state reset.

   Clears the upper half of every vector register. What it is for is the state a processor is in when
   it executes a *legacy-encoded* SSE instruction while some upper half is non-zero, which costs a
   large and silent penalty on every part this matters on - so a function whose body is encoded
   without a VEX prefix wants this to have happened before its first instruction runs.

   **`v3` and not `x64`**, because the instruction is AVX's: below that tier there is no encoding
   that could have left an upper half non-zero, nothing to clear, and no instruction to write. A
   level selector is answered as "at least", so this exists on a v3 or a v4 build and nowhere else.
   `avx` is not a spelling for it here for the reason it is not one on the command line either - AVX
   alone was Sandy Bridge, which this compiler describes as v2.

   **A call rather than something the backend emits at the entry of such a function**, and the reason
   is that the two are about different sets of functions. `@x86_legacy_sse` is about *encoding* and
   belongs on every function of a region; clearing the state may only happen where no vector value is
   arriving, which is the region's entry points and not the helpers below them. A rule deriving one
   from the other would read a signature and be right until somebody added a vector parameter, at
   which point it would stop firing and say nothing.

   **It is destructive, and where it is safe is the caller's convention.** Every register it clears
   has to be one nobody is holding a value in: `@convention(clobber)` is what says so for the caller,
   since nothing survives one of its calls, and having no vector parameter is what says so for this
   function. Written anywhere else it is corruption of a value nothing named.

   On LLVM this is dropped - that backend chooses its own encodings and places its own resets. See
   Analysis-Warts.md §7 and `legacyVectorEncodings` in compiler/codegen/x64/target.h.
-}
@platform(v3) pub fn X86.vzeroupper() -> {}

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The SHA extension - `@platform(sha)`, seven instructions.
   ==========================================================================================

   Not inside any level: a v3 or v4 machine may or may not have these, so `sha` is a selector of its
   own and `-enable-inst sha` or a host that reports it is what turns them on. `nosha` is the other
   half of that partition and is what `Digest/Hardware.nosha.yana` is selected by; see
   `targetSelector`, where the pair is argued.

   **`sha` also requires v3**, which is a fact about the library rather than about the silicon:
   `Digest/Hardware.sha.yana` is a legacy-encoded region and is therefore written in terms of
   `X86.vzeroupper()` above. Goldmont shipped these instructions with no AVX at all; such a part is
   `nosha` here and takes the portable compression, which is the whole of what it gives up.

   **Why the operands say `Vec(U32, 4)`.** Not `Vec(U32)`, whose lane count is the target's, and not
   a const parameter: these instructions read 128 bits as the four words a SHA state or message
   schedule is made of, there is no other width, and a signature that suggested otherwise would be
   describing an instruction that does not exist.

   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   SHA-1, four instructions.

   `sha1Message1(w0_3, w4_7)` and `sha1Message2(w16_19, w13_16)` are the two halves of the message
   schedule: the first performs the exclusive-ors that do not need a rotation, the second finishes
   them and rotates. Between them a caller does the one `xor` the pair does not - the schedule needs
   four inputs and each instruction reads two.

   `sha1NextE(e, state)` adds the `E` working variable into the top word of the next round group,
   which is the one part of SHA-1's state that does not live in the four-word register with the rest.

   `sha1Rounds(abcd, e, group)` performs four rounds. `group` selects which of the four round
   functions - 0 for the choice, 1 and 3 for the parity, 2 for the majority - and **must be a
   literal**: it is the instruction's trailing byte, so a computed one is not an instruction. It is
   checked where it is written, which is the only place that can name the argument.
-}
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Message1(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Message2(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1NextE(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha1Rounds(abcd: Vec(U32, 4), e: Vec(U32, 4), group: Int) -> Vec(U32, 4)

{-
   SHA-256, three instructions.

   `sha256Message1(w0_3, w4_7)` and `sha256Message2(w16_19, w12_15)` are the message schedule's two
   halves, on SHA-1's pattern: the first applies the small sigma to four words, the second finishes
   the sum and applies the other sigma. The `+ w[i-7]` in the middle is the caller's, and it is one
   `add` and one `alignLanes`.

   `sha256Rounds(state, feed, keys)` performs **two full rounds**. `keys` is the two message words
   with their round constants already added, and on the machine it is an implicit operand read out of
   `xmm0` - which is a register-allocation constraint the backend carries and a caller never sees.
   `state` and `feed` are the eight working variables in two registers, in the order the instruction
   wants them rather than in the order the specification names them: `{C, D, G, H}` and
   `{A, B, E, F}`, which is why the compression function shuffles once on the way in and once on the
   way out.
-}
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Message1(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Message2(a: Vec(U32, 4), b: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
@platform(sha) pub fn X86.sha256Rounds(state: Vec(U32, 4), feed: Vec(U32, 4), keys: Vec(U32, 4)) -> Vec(U32, 4)
